  "body": "## What changed\n\nThis PR is the mesh-body slice of #3643. A tool component body can now come from a STEP, STL or OBJ file. The body is placed by its own ISO 13399 mounting frame. All code is domain code in `camcraft-geometry` and `camcraft-tools`. There is no new crate edge, no app change and no UI change.\n\n- `camcraft_geometry::step_csys`: `MCS_INSERT` is now `CsysRole::Mounting`. The app's `step_csys.rs` re-exports this type, so the app reads it too. A file that names both `MCS` and `MCS_INSERT` has two mounting frames, and `ToolCsysReport::find` refuses it as ambiguous.\n- New `camcraft_tools::mesh_body::MeshBody`:\n  - STEP: meshed by the existing kernel import (`camcraft_geometry::step::parse`) at a 0.01 mm chord, with its named coordinate systems.\n  - STL (ASCII and binary) and OBJ: read by the new `camcraft_tools::mesh_file`, with frames that the caller stores (`with_frames`).\n  - `parts()` reads the ports (CSW, CSWx, CSWx_y) and the CIP in the mounting frame (MCS or MCS_INSERT). A port or CIP that is not below the mounting frame is refused by name, because the MCS +Z must point toward the machine.\n  - `spindle_geometry()` gives a `ComponentGeometry` with a `ComponentBody`: the revolved envelope of all facets, drawn as a stepped outline. The outline only grows the envelope, and a last check refuses a body whose outline does not enclose the envelope.\n  - `lathe_geometry()` gives the ports, the CIP and a `LatheBody` for `resolve_lathe`. All bodies of the stack go to `LatheClearance::of` as one set, as #4194 does.\n- Never smaller than the steel: a meshed STEP body grows by the certified chord deviation of the kernel. The spindle bands grow by it. The lathe clearance grows through the new `LatheBody::padded` and `LatheClearance::padded` (`lathe_clearance_pad.rs`).\n- Facet budget: one component body has at most 1 000 000 facets. Past the budget, the body is refused by name, and the text names #1041. A STEP model is first meshed coarser by the existing importer.\n- Corrupt or degenerate input gives a named refusal (`MeshBodyError`, `MeshFileError`), never a panic.\n- `Frame::unmap_point`, `unmap_dir` and `relative` are new (the inverse of the frame map).\n- Decision: `docs/decisions/3643-mesh-bodies.md`.\n\n## Pinning tests\n\n- `camcraft-geometry` `step_csys_read::mcs_insert_names_the_mounting_frame_and_two_mounts_are_ambiguous`\n- `camcraft-tools` lib, `mesh_body_tests`:\n  - `an_stl_holder_with_stored_frames_resolves_in_a_spindle_stack` (gauge length and band of a stack with an end mill)\n  - `ascii_and_binary_stl_and_obj_read_the_same_facets`\n  - `a_step_body_places_by_its_own_iso_13399_frames` (MCS and MCS_INSERT, deviation grow)\n  - `lathe_bands_grow_by_the_pad`\n  - `the_drawn_outline_never_falls_inside_the_envelope`\n  - `a_missing_doubled_or_upside_down_frame_is_refused_by_name`\n  - `degenerate_and_over_budget_files_are_refused_by_name`\n  - `a_corrupt_step_body_is_refused_by_name`\n  - `mangled_files_never_panic` (a small fuzz pass over STL, OBJ and STEP)\n- `camcraft-tools` lib, `lathe_assembly_tests::an_stl_holder_mesh_gives_the_box_holder_clearance` (the same clearance as the box holder, and the pad grow through `resolve_lathe`)\n\n## Gate results (head d1eb8a1bcc8e37b31dca7b0679951a2a2659ef44)\n\n- Windows (winbuild), log `C:\\camcraft-logs\\gate-3643d-d1eb8a1bcc8e37b31dca7b0679951a2a2659ef44.log`, first line `head d1eb8a1bcc8e37b31dca7b0679951a2a2659ef44`:\n  - `cargo xtask gate quick --base origin/main`: the diff touches `crates/geometry`, so quick falls back to batch. 1229 steps, 0 failed, 0 stalled (152.6 min).\n  - `cargo clippy -p camcraft-tools -p camcraft-geometry --all-targets -- -D warnings`: clean.\n  - `cargo xtask gate light`: 6 steps, 0 failed, 0 stalled.\n  - `cargo xtask gate heavy --base origin/main --dry-run`: the diff reaches the app and motion heavy lanes through `camcraft-geometry`. It does not reach the kernel heavy lane. No heavy step ran (P6).\n- macOS: `cargo xtask gate quick --base origin/main` (CAMCRAFT_GATE_JOBS=5, batch fallback): 1229 steps, 0 failed, 0 stalled (76.4 min). Strict clippy on `camcraft-tools` and `camcraft-geometry`: clean. Log `gate-3643d-mac-d1eb8a1bcc8e37b31dca7b0679951a2a2659ef44.log`, first line `head d1eb8a1bcc8e37b31dca7b0679951a2a2659ef44`.\n\n## Not exercised\n\n- No app wiring: no document field, command, MCP tool or UI for a mesh body yet. The callers in `crates/app/src/tool_assembly*.rs` are not changed, because open PRs #4249 and #4278 change those files.\n- No vendor file was used. The STEP fixture is our own `testdata/models/tool_csys_box_iso13399.step`, changed in the test. The STL and OBJ files are made in the tests.\n- The full heavy lanes (they run at the cut).\n- The example catalog suites: no post or toolpath changed.\n\n## Remaining on #3643\n\n- App wiring of `MeshBody` (document field for the file and its stored frames, commands with MCP tools, the call in the app resolvers).\n- The boring-bar overhang in a sleeve.\n- #1041: a coarser mesh in place of the over-budget refusal.\n\nChangelog: Tool components can take their body from a STEP, STL or OBJ file, placed by the file's own ISO 13399 mounting frame (MCS or MCS_INSERT); the judged body is never smaller than the mesh (library level, no UI yet).\n\nEffort: high\n\nRefs #3643\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n",
